1
00:00:09,092 --> 00:00:11,092
All right.

2
00:00:12,997 --> 00:00:17,621
Module and this one is going to be
about creating a gateway with Linux.

3
00:00:17,621 --> 00:00:19,730
Now we've heard the term gateway.

4
00:00:19,730 --> 00:00:25,146
If you recall that was
the term used in that 1970.

5
00:00:25,146 --> 00:00:30,227
I think it was 1974 paper and
it defined this gateway in

6
00:00:30,227 --> 00:00:35,722
the context of the Internet protocol
as a router that allows for

7
00:00:35,722 --> 00:00:40,097
the connection between
two different networks.

8
00:00:40,097 --> 00:00:44,112
So we're going to kind of take that
definition and extend it a little bit.

9
00:00:44,112 --> 00:00:48,428
We're going to define it as something
that sits at the edge of the network and

10
00:00:48,428 --> 00:00:52,490
provides some functionality beyond
just routing and forwarding.

11
00:00:52,490 --> 00:00:56,274
So we're going to go deeper
into the depths of what

12
00:00:56,274 --> 00:00:59,032
Linux networking can provide.

13
00:00:59,032 --> 00:01:03,837
So this may be the public
Internet might be on one side and

14
00:01:03,837 --> 00:01:07,910
the private network on the other side.

15
00:01:07,910 --> 00:01:12,504
Or the edge might be kind of
between maybe two segments of

16
00:01:12,504 --> 00:01:16,630
a network under the same
administrative domain.

17
00:01:17,850 --> 00:01:22,001
So what functionality are we
going to be looking at and

18
00:01:22,001 --> 00:01:28,350
what constitutes things that you
might see in a gateway of a network.

19
00:01:28,350 --> 00:01:33,398
So the first is filtering and
some address translation.

20
00:01:33,398 --> 00:01:37,975
So this allows you to have a policy for
what traffic is allowed in and

21
00:01:37,975 --> 00:01:39,465
out of your network.

22
00:01:39,465 --> 00:01:44,072
And possibly translating addresses,
allowing you to keep private

23
00:01:44,072 --> 00:01:48,536
addresses internally with a public
facing address separately.

24
00:01:51,643 --> 00:01:55,677
Next, functionality might be
something like load balancing.

25
00:01:55,677 --> 00:02:00,538
So say we're running
a replicated service where each

26
00:02:00,538 --> 00:02:05,399
of these hosts can perform
the same functionality and

27
00:02:05,399 --> 00:02:12,109
we have it replicated so that we can
handle more requests at the same time.

28
00:02:12,109 --> 00:02:17,479
So load balancing enables
forwarding to the back end

29
00:02:17,479 --> 00:02:24,078
servers to choose a specific
back end server algorithmically.

30
00:02:24,078 --> 00:02:28,628
And then we can balance the load
by sending different requests to

31
00:02:28,628 --> 00:02:30,151
different servers.

32
00:02:30,151 --> 00:02:35,349
And then the last one we're going to
cover is quality of service.

33
00:02:35,349 --> 00:02:40,029
And so
this enables you to have this policy that

34
00:02:40,029 --> 00:02:44,834
describes the limits and
priority on traffic.

35
00:02:44,834 --> 00:02:49,184
And as you could guess,
Linux covers them all.

36
00:02:49,184 --> 00:02:53,772
So we have tools like iptables, ipvs,

37
00:02:53,772 --> 00:02:59,201
tc that covers each of
these different areas.

38
00:02:59,201 --> 00:03:04,180
And so for each of them we're going to
cover first some foundational

39
00:03:04,180 --> 00:03:08,630
background to explain what is
the functionality we want.

40
00:03:08,630 --> 00:03:14,729
Then we're going to go into
an overview of the Linux utility and

41
00:03:14,729 --> 00:03:16,889
how to make use of it.

42
00:03:16,889 --> 00:03:23,438
And for each we're going to provide
practice exercises in a github repo.

43
00:03:23,438 --> 00:03:27,180
Now it's not just enough to have that and

44
00:03:27,180 --> 00:03:31,596
then execute those
commands in that exercise.

45
00:03:31,596 --> 00:03:37,706
The goal would be for you to take that and
use that as a platform to then go

46
00:03:37,706 --> 00:03:43,630
a little further to really explore
the ins and outs of each of these.

47
00:03:43,630 --> 00:03:48,840
So with that we're going to end here and
start this up next time.

48
00:03:48,840 --> 00:03:50,583
All right, thanks.